==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|meretrīcius|lang=la}}, from {{term|meretrīx||harlot, prostitute|lang=la}}, from {{term|mereō||earn, deserve, merit|lang=la}} (English {{term|merit|lang=en}}) + {{term|-trīx||(female agent)|lang=la}} (English {{term|-trix|lang=en}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌmɛrɪˈtrɪʃəs/|/ˌmɛrəˈtrɪʃəs/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-meretricious.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪʃəs|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} Of, or relating to [[prostitute]]s or [[prostitution]].
# {{context|legal|lang=en}} Involving unlawful sexual connection or lack of consent by at least one party (said of a romantic relationship)
# [[tastelessly|Tastelessly]] [[gaudy]]; superficially attractive but having in reality no value or substance; falsely alluring.
#* {{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=10
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=He looked round the poor room, at the distempered walls, and the bad engravings in '''meretricious''' frames, the crinkly paper and wax flowers on the chiffonier; and he thought of a room like Father Bryan's, with panelling, with cut glass, with tulips in silver pots, such a room as he had hoped to have for his own.}}
#* '''2006''', {{w|Clive James}}, ''North Face of Soho'', Picador 2007, p. 164:
#*: When I lifted my eyes from the page, there was none of the '''meretricious''' argument London always offers that the sole real purpose in life is to hustle for a buck.

